course: everyday
lesson: 388
title: How to say stop
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Lily stood in front of the bathroom mirror and said, «Stop. I don't like that.» It came out as a squeak. She tried again, standing tall, looking herself in the eye, saying it slowly and loudly. She felt silly, but she kept practising. The next morning, when Tom tugged her ponytail in the line, she turned round and said it exactly like in the mirror. Tom let go at once.
explain: The day after, Tom tried again and Lily said «stop». «You're no fun,» he moaned. Lily wobbled for a second, then said: «I'm fun, but this isn't.» A «stop» doesn't need a long reason; it is reason enough. When another boy pretended not to hear her, she said it louder, then walked straight to Mrs Okafor. «I said stop and he didn't,» she told her, worried it was telling tales. «When someone doesn't stop,» said Mrs Okafor, «that's exactly when a grown-up helps.»
explain: Stop works for other people too. When a boy flicked Sam's ear, Sam said «stop» in a shaky voice, and his friends said it with him: «Stop.» The boy stopped. Maddie, who usually shoved first, tried saying «stop» when a boy poked her, and it worked, and for once she didn't end up in the head's office. Chloe practised a big strong «no» with her sister, and when Maddie came near her plaits again, Chloe said «no» and Maddie backed off.
explain: The bravest stop of the week came from Tom himself. His friends dared him to pull Lily's hair again, and he said: «Stop it, I'm not doing that.» They were so surprised that they stopped daring him. In the end the class made a rule and stuck it on the wall: «When someone says stop, we stop.»
fact: «Stop» or «no» is a complete answer; it does not need a good reason.
fact: Saying stop clearly works best: stand tall, look at the person, use a steady, loud voice.
fact: Practising a stop at home makes it come out strong when you need it.
fact: A stop is stronger when friends repeat it.
fact: Saying stop is often better than pushing, and it doesn't get you into trouble.
fact: If someone doesn't stop, the next step is to move away and tell an adult.
fact: Telling an adult that someone ignored your stop is not telling tales.
fact: Pretending not to hear a stop is still not stopping.
fact: You can say stop to your own friends when they dare you to do something unkind.
fact: When someone says stop to you, the right thing is to stop at once, even in a game.
exercise: Why did Lily practise «stop, I don't like that» in the mirror? | So it would come out strong when she needed it | Because she wanted to be an actress on the telly | Because her mum told her off | So she could shout at her little brother
exercise: How did Lily feel while practising? | Silly but determined | Angry and ready to fight | Proud and showing off | Bored and nearly asleep
exercise: What happened the next time Tom tugged her hair? | She said stop loudly and he stopped | She pulled his hair back to get even | She cried and ran home | She told him she liked it
exercise: Tom said «you're no fun». What did Lily answer? | «I'm fun, but this isn't» | «Fine, carry on then, I don't mind» | «Sorry, I'll stop saying stop» | «You're no fun either, idiot»
exercise: Why doesn't a «stop» need a long reason? | Not liking something is reason enough | Because nobody listens to reasons | Because reasons are for adults only | Stop only works with a reason
exercise: How did Sam feel when he said stop to the boy flicking his ear? | Nervous | Furious with him | Calm and bored | Excited and happy
exercise: Why did the boy stop flicking Sam's ear? | Sam's friends repeated «stop» with him | Sam threatened to hit him | The bell rang at that very moment and saved him | The boy's finger got tired
exercise: What usually happened when Maddie pushed instead of talking? | She ended up in trouble herself | The boys always said sorry | The teacher gave her a prize | Nothing at all happened
exercise: What happened when Maddie said «stop» instead of pushing? | The boy stopped and she stayed out of trouble | The boy laughed and kept on poking her harder | Maddie was sent to the head | Maddie felt weak and gave up
exercise: Lily was worried telling Mrs Okafor was telling tales. What did Mrs Okafor say? | When someone doesn't stop, a grown-up helps | Sort it out between yourselves next time, please | Only tell me if there is blood | You should have said it more nicely
exercise: A boy pretended not to hear Lily's stop. What did she do? | Said it louder, then went to the teacher | Gave up and let him carry on poking | Pushed him over | Said sorry for interrupting him and went back to her work
exercise: How did Chloe get ready to say no? | She practised a big strong «no» with her sister | She stayed home from school for the whole week | She asked Maddie to promise first | She cut off her plaits so nobody could pull them
exercise: Tom's friends dared him to pull Lily's hair again. What did he say? | «Stop it, I'm not doing that» | «OK, but only once» | «You do it, I'm scared» | «Only if you give me a sweet»
exercise: Why was Tom's stop to his friends brave? | It is hard to say no to your own friends | It got him a reward from the teacher at assembly | His friends were much smaller than him | Nobody heard him say it
exercise: What rule did the class make? | When someone says stop, we stop | Only teachers may say stop | Stop only counts if you say please | Say stop three times before it counts
exercise: In a tickling game your friend shouts «stop!» but laughs. What should you do? | Stop, and check if they really want to go on | Keep going because they're laughing, so it's fine | Tickle harder to make them laugh more | Stop only if an adult is looking
test: «oi stop flicking my ear» — he does it again. what next? | Move away and tell a teacher | Flick him back, but a lot harder | Let him, he'll get bored soon | Pretend it doesn't hurt one bit
test: «ur no fun, it's just a joke». Does Lily have to put up with it? | No, a stop is reason enough | Yes, jokes are allowed to hurt a bit | Yes, if he says it's only a joke | Only if she can explain why to him
test: Maddie, fists ready, boy poking her arm again. What's the move that keeps her out of trouble? | Say «stop» loud and clear | Shove him into the bushes | Poke him back twice | Kick his bag across the yard
test: «ur a grass if u tell miss». Lily said stop and he didn't. Is telling right? | Yes, that's when a grown-up should help | No, she should never tell on anyone | No, she should sort it out with her fists | Yes, but only if he hits her
test: Your mates: «go on, pull her hair, dare ya». What's a strong answer? | «Stop it, I'm not doing that» | «Fine, but you go first» | «Later, when Miss isn't looking» | «Only if she doesn't see»
test: Sam says «stop» but his voice wobbles. You're standing next to him. Most useful thing? | Say «stop» with him | Laugh at his wobbly voice | Walk away quickly without a word | Tell him to hit the boy
test: «he didn't hear me» — he's grinning. What's true? | Pretending not to hear a stop is still not stopping | If he didn't hear it, then it's her own fault | She should whisper it again, more politely | Stop only counts if he answers
test: Chloe's sister: «say it again, louder, look at me!» Why is this good practice? | A practised no comes out strong when needed | It's a game for bossy big sisters who like shouting | It helps her sing better | It's to make her sister go away
test: Pillow fight, your little cousin yells «STOP» but he's giggling. Best move? | Stop and ask if he wants to keep going | Keep going, he's laughing, so he likes it | Pin him down for a joke | Stop only when he cries
test: Teacher: «What does our wall rule say?» Which is it? | When someone says stop, we stop | When the teacher says stop, we stop | Stop only counts in lessons | Stop means slow down a bit
exam: Identify the course of action prescribed for a pupil whose verbal request to desist has been disregarded. | Withdrawal from the situation and notification of a responsible adult | Physical retaliation proportionate to the provocation received | Continued toleration of the conduct in silence | Repetition of the request in a whisper
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the status of the word «stop» when uttered by one pupil to another? | It is a sufficient reason in itself and requires no further justification | It is valid only when accompanied by a satisfactory explanation of one's reasons | It may be disregarded where the conduct is intended as a jest | It is binding only when uttered by a member of staff
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the efficacy of a refusal voiced by several bystanders. | A refusal repeated by companions is more likely to be heeded | A refusal is weakened by the participation of others | Bystanders ought to refrain from any involvement whatsoever | A refusal is effective only when accompanied by force
exam: It is given that a pupil is incited by his companions to repeat an unkind act and declines. State the most accurate characterisation of his conduct. | An act of courage, since refusal before one's own companions is difficult | An act of disloyalty towards his companions | An act of no significance whatsoever, the dare being trivial and harmless | An act of timidity, indicating a want of spirit
